        "status": "complete",
        "truncated": false,
        "usage": "provider calls and cost unknown; not included in router savings"
      },
      "final_response": "**File: `queue.py`, class `EventQueue`**\n\n- `record(event)` (lines 5-6): appends `event` to `self.pending`.\n- `flush(sink)` (lines 8-12):\n  1. Snapshots `self.pending` into a new tuple `batch`.\n  2. Calls `sink.write_batch(batch)`.\n  3. Only *after* that call returns does it clear `self.pending`.\n  4. Returns `len(batch)`.\n\n**Flush ordering:** snapshot \u2192 write \u2192 clear. The clear is not in a `finally` block and happens strictly after `write_batch` returns successfully.\n\n**If `sink.write_batch` raises:**\n- The exception propagates up out of `flush()` uncaught \u2014 there's no try/except.\n- `self.pending.clear()` is never reached, so the events are **not dropped**: they remain in `self.pending` exactly as they were before the flush attempt.\n- However, `batch` was built from a copied tuple of `self.pending` at the time of the call, so nothing was lost or duplicated \u2014 the queue is left in the same state as if `flush` had never been called, aside from whatever partial side effects `write_batch` itself performed before raising (e.g., a sink that writes some events then throws could produce duplicates on a later retry, since this code has no way to know how much of the batch was actually persisted).\n- Net effect: safe for full-batch failures (simple retry works), but not idempotent against partial-write failures inside `sink.write_batch`.",
